(203006, 11631, 5898, '<td valign="top">NIce depth of field picture, a little out of focus in the front of the picture, that''s is the cameras fault not yours. The biggest problem happened when you tilted your camera to the side to get a vertical picture it put your flash to the side. This cause all your shaddows to be to the right of the images. If have a detachable flash and an extion cord that connects to the camera you can hand hold the flash above the camera and all your shadows will be behind the image. There are also grips you can buy that can attach to your camera that holds the flash. The parts that holds the flash swivals to always have the flash on top. I hope this helps. John</td>', 'NIce depth of field picture, a little out of focus in the front of the picture, that''s is the cameras fault not yours. The biggest problem happened when you tilted your camera to the side to get a vertical picture it put your flash to the side. This cause all your shaddows to be to the right of the images. If have a detachable flash and an extion cord that connects to the camera you can hand hold the flash above the camera and all your shadows will be behind the image. There are also grips you can buy that can attach to your camera that holds the flash. The parts that holds the flash swivals to always have the flash on top. I hope this helps. John', '2003-01-29 10:52:39.000000', NULL, 1),
(203008, 11644, 5898, '<td valign="top">Nice image and I like the way the light shines throught the doors. However, the image is too dark to see all the details. If you were to go up to the wall and take your reading, (pressing down slightly on your shutter release) continue to hold down the release until you get back to were you want to take the shot. then shot the picture. Your camera will read what light it takes to get the wall and not the sky. This will give you a very satisfying photograph. I hope this helped. John</td>', 'Nice image and I like the way the light shines throught the doors. However, the image is too dark to see all the details. If you were to go up to the wall and take your reading, (pressing down slightly on your shutter release) continue to hold down the release until you get back to were you want to take the shot. then shot the picture. Your camera will read what light it takes to get the wall and not the sky. This will give you a very satisfying photograph. I hope this helped. John', '2003-01-29 10:57:05.000000', NULL, 1),
(203009, 11524, 5898, '<td valign="top">I assume you used a fisheye lens to get this image. Very creative and used some advanced thinking skills. Let me know if you created this in a different way. John Gill</td>', 'I assume you used a fisheye lens to get this image. Very creative and used some advanced thinking skills. Let me know if you created this in a different way. John Gill', '2003-01-29 10:59:24.000000', NULL, 1),
(203010, 11623, 5898, '<td valign="top">Nice colors, the perspective, shapes and forms, lines, and patterns all help to make this a good looking photo. What you need is something in front of the wll to help give it a more three dimensional look. Perhaps a cat, an old lady walking by, etc. The plants help a little. Good luck, John Gill</td>', 'Nice colors, the perspective, shapes and forms, lines, and patterns all help to make this a good looking photo. What you need is something in front of the wll to help give it a more three dimensional look. Perhaps a cat, an old lady walking by, etc. The plants help a little. Good luck, John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:03:03.000000', NULL, 1),
(203011, 11464, 5898, '<td valign="top">Nice colors, focus, etc. It just needs something to make it stand out in my opinion. Perhaps a small child in front of the door, thet would not take up much space and would give the picture a 3-D look. Who knows? Good Luck. John Gill</td>', 'Nice colors, focus, etc. It just needs something to make it stand out in my opinion. Perhaps a small child in front of the door, thet would not take up much space and would give the picture a 3-D look. Who knows? Good Luck. John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:07:12.000000', NULL, 1),
(203012, 11651, 5898, '<td valign="top">Very nice image,color, and point of view. The only thing that is distracting is the light glare in the bottom left corner of the alter area. Excellent picture I hope you do well. John Gill</td>', 'Very nice image,color, and point of view. The only thing that is distracting is the light glare in the bottom left corner of the alter area. Excellent picture I hope you do well. John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:09:38.000000', NULL, 1),
(203014, 11511, 3724, '<td valign="top">I scored this high......  i enjoy your choice of colour - the red white &amp;blue.  The selection of the elements contained in your design have been balanced flawlessly and establish formal structure which is intriguing and also agreeable.  I like the dark round windows at the top......  the architectural image is transformed into a hard-edged cubic face with eyes, nose, mouth and white beard!!!!</td>', 'I scored this high......  i enjoy your choice of colour - the red white &blue.  The selection of the elements contained in your design have been balanced flawlessly and establish formal structure which is intriguing and also agreeable.  I like the dark round windows at the top......  the architectural image is transformed into a hard-edged cubic face with eyes, nose, mouth and white beard!!!!', '2003-01-29 11:12:20.000000', NULL, 1),
(203015, 11612, 5898, '<td valign="top">Very nice!!! I like the use of the two images, the cloths, hair, and stance reminds you of a time long ago. This goes well with the old window. Excellent (10) John Gill</td>', 'Very nice!!! I like the use of the two images, the cloths, hair, and stance reminds you of a time long ago. This goes well with the old window. Excellent (10) John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:13:41.000000', NULL, 1),
(203016, 11387, 5898, '<td valign="top">Excellent take on the challenge. One of the things I challenge my students with is to make their pictures look 3 dimensional. And never take a picture straight on, try to find a piont of view that is more creative. i.e. shooting up-down-or through something. A suggestion might be to put a potted plant in front of the door. New verses old and it would give it dimension. Just a thought. (8) John Gill</td>', 'Excellent take on the challenge. One of the things I challenge my students with is to make their pictures look 3 dimensional. And never take a picture straight on, try to find a piont of view that is more creative. i.e. shooting up-down-or through something. A suggestion might be to put a potted plant in front of the door. New verses old and it would give it dimension. Just a thought. (8) John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:20:19.000000', NULL, 1),
(203017, 11431, 5898, '<td valign="top">I like the fact that you shot from the inside instead of outside like most of the other photos. The lighting really makes a difference and helps the image. (8) Jojn Gill</td>', 'I like the fact that you shot from the inside instead of outside like most of the other photos. The lighting really makes a difference and helps the image. (8) Jojn Gill', '2003-01-29 11:22:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(203019, 11362, 5898, '<td valign="top">Excellent take on the challenge and very creative. One point off for shooting straight at the object, even a slight angle would be more creative. Good Luck, John Gill</td>', 'Excellent take on the challenge and very creative. One point off for shooting straight at the object, even a slight angle would be more creative. Good Luck, John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:25:52.000000', NULL, 1),
(203020, 11402, 5898, '<td valign="top">Nice point of view, and depth of field. You must have a very good camera. If you could have taken it at a different time of day you could have gotten some really neat shadows. Good luck. John Gill</td>', 'Nice point of view, and depth of field. You must have a very good camera. If you could have taken it at a different time of day you could have gotten some really neat shadows. Good luck. John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:28:12.000000', NULL, 1),
(203024, 11629, 5898, '<td valign="top">The dog in the window never fails. It tells a story, creates emotion, and gives the picture a 3 dementional look. Many of my students have used this theme for their work. The picture taken from the outside can mean the dog is waiting for you and creates a very desireable image. Nice job, (8) John Gill</td>', 'The dog in the window never fails. It tells a story, creates emotion, and gives the picture a 3 dementional look. Many of my students have used this theme for their work. The picture taken from the outside can mean the dog is waiting for you and creates a very desireable image. Nice job, (8) John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:32:48.000000', NULL, 1),
(203026, 11511, 5898, '<td valign="top">You met the challenge and the door has some appeal. However, if you could have taken the picture at an angle, (shooting up, down, or perhaps through something) you could have had a more desireable image. John Gill</td>', 'You met the challenge and the door has some appeal. However, if you could have taken the picture at an angle, (shooting up, down, or perhaps through something) you could have had a more desireable image. John Gill', '2003-01-29 11:34:56.000000', NULL, 1),
